ge 3: Differentiable Monte Carlo Tree Search with active node expansion L36:     7. cite26†3.7 Joint training: multi-objective neuro-symbolic active learning L37:   5. cite27†4 Experiments L38:     1. cite28†4.1 Datasets L39:     2. cite29†4.2 Baselines and evaluation protocol L40:     3. cite30†4.3 Implementation details L41:     4. cite31†4.4 Performance comparison L42:     5. cite32†4.5 Ablation study L43:     6. cite33†4.6 Structure-encoder comparison L44:     7. cite34†4.7 Case study L45:     8. cite35†4.8 Compatibility with different LLM backbones L46:     9. cite36†4.9 Efficiency and stability L47:     10. cite37†4.10 Active Supervision Efficiency and Query Economy L48:     11. cite38†4.11 Training Dynamics and Convergence Behavior L49:     12. cite39†4.12 Search Topology and Progressive Widening Analysis L50:     13. cite40†4.13 Error Analysis and Failure Mode Characterization L51:     14. cite41†4.14 Hyperparameter Sensitivity Analysis L52:       1. cite42†Effect of the human-cost penalty $\\beta$. L53:       2. cite43†Gumbel temperature $\\tau$ and search dynamics. L54:       3. cite44†Balancing multi-objective weights $\\lambda_{1:3}$. L55:       4. cite45†Deployment recommendations. L56:     15. cite46†4.15 Summary L57:   6. cite47†5 Conclusion L58:   7. cite48†References L59:   8. cite49†A Theoretical Analysis L60:     1. cite50†A.1 Preliminaries and assumptions L61:     2. cite51†A.2 Convergence of the inner loop: differentiable MCTS L62:     3. cite52†A.3 Uncertainty calibration guarantees L63:     4. cite53†A.4 Budget-aware query complexity for active human queries L64:     5. cite54†A.5 Soft MCTS approximation and low-temperature limit L65:     6. cite55†A.6 Neural-symbolic fusion consistency L66:     7. cite56†A.7 Discussion and priorities for formal refinement L67: cite57†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L68: \nL69: arXiv:2602.15353v1 [cs.CL] 17 Feb 2026\nL70: # NeuroSymActive: Differentiable Neural-Symbolic Reasoning with Active Exploration for Knowledge Graph Question Answering\nL71: Rong Fu ^{†}^{†}thanks: Corresponding author: mc46603@um.edu.mo Affiliation: University of Macau Email: mc46603@um.edu.mo    Yang Li Affiliation: University of Chinese Academy of Sciences Email: liyang221@mails.ucas.ac.cn    Zeyu Zhang Affiliation: The Australian National University Email: steve.zeyu.zhang@outlook.com    Jiekai Wu Affiliation: Juntendo University Email: ketsu0612@gmail.com    Yaohua Liu Affiliation: Guangdong Institute of Intelligence Science and Technology Email: liuyaohua@gdiist.cn    Shuaishuai Cao Affiliation: Central South University Email: 202226204053@jxnu.edu.cn    Yangchen Zeng Affiliation: Southeast University Email: 220245765@seu.edu.cn    Yuhang Zhang Affiliation: China Agricultural University Email: zyh140981@outlook.com    Xiaojing Du Affiliation: Adelaide University Email: xiaojing.du@adelaide.edu.au    Chuang Zhao Affiliation: The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology Email: czhaobo@connect.ust.hk    Kangning Cui Affiliation: Wake Forest University Email: cuij@wfu.edu    Simon Fong Affiliation: University of Macau Email: ccfong@um.edu.mo\nL72: ###### Abstract\nL73: Large pretrained language models and neural reasoning systems have advanced many natural language tasks, yet they remain challenged by knowledge-intensive queries that require precise, structured multi-hop inference.\nL74: Knowledge graphs provide a compact symbolic substrate for factual grounding, but integrating graph structure with neural models is nontrivial: naively embedding graph facts into prompts leads to inefficiency and fragility, while purely symbolic or search-heavy approaches can be costly in retrievals and lack gradient-based refinement.\nL75: We introduce NeuroSymActive, a modular framework that combines a differentiable neural-symbolic reasoning layer with an active, value-guided exploration controller for Knowledge Graph Question Answering. The method couples soft-unification style symbolic modules with a neural path evaluator and a Monte-Carlo style exploration policy that prioritizes high-value path expansions.\nL76: Empirical results on standard KGQA benchmarks show that NeuroSymActive attains strong answer accuracy while reducing the number of expensive graph lookups and model calls compared to common retrieval-augmented baselines.\nL77: \nL78: \nL79: Keywords Knowledge Graph Question Answering; neural-symbolic reasoning; differentiable logic; active exploration; Monte Carlo search\nL80: ## 1 Introduction\nL81: Large language models have produced striking improvements in many language tasks, but they do not always provide robust solutions for questions that require structured, multi-hop reasoning over world knowledge. Knowledge graphs are a natural vehicle for encoding factual relations and multi-step semantic chains, yet incorporating graphs into neural pipelines raises several practical and methodological issues.\nL82: One common strategy retrieves relevant subgraphs and injects them into neural models or prompts; this approach grounds model predictions but can be inefficient, since iterative retrieval and repeated model calls are often necessary to assemble multi-hop evidence [cite58†1 , cite59†2 , cite60†3 ].\nL83: Another line of work embeds graph structure into differentiable modules or applies soft logical operators to enable gradient-based refinement; these methods improve interpretability and allow end-to-end training, but they can struggle to discover long-range compositional paths without targeted search guidance [cite61†4 , cite62†5 , cite63†6 ]. Symbolic search and beam-style expansion offer exhaustive path coverage but at the cost of many KG lookups and brittle heuristics [cite64†7 , cite65†8 ].\nL84: Recent attempts to combine learning and search point to the benefit of guiding exploration with learned value estimates, but existing instantiations have not fully reconciled differentiable symbolic constraints with principled active exploration [cite66†9 , cite67†10 ].\nL85: Motivated by these gaps, we present NeuroSymActive, a framework that tightly integrates three components. The first is a differentiable neural-symbolic reasoning layer that supports soft pattern matching and differentiable rule scoring, which allows logical preferences to be learned from data while retaining interpretability. The second is a neural path evaluator that assigns value estimates to partial reasoning trajectories.\nL86: The third is an active exploration controller that uses the evaluator to prioritize expansion and rollout, thereby focusing retrieval effort on promising branches and reducing unnecessary KG queries. This design intentionally separates offline learning of symbolic affinities from online search control, enabling efficient and adaptive multi-hop reasoning under constrained retrieval budgets.\nL87: Our contributions are as follows. Our first contribution is a compact differentiable neural-symbolic module for KG reasoning that supports soft-unification, differentiable scoring of rule-like patterns, and seamless interfacing with neural path evaluators. Our second contribution is an active exploration mechanism that integrates learned value signals into a Monte Carlo style search procedure; this mechanism concentrates retrieval and expansion on high-payoff paths and reduces the number of costly lookups.\nL88: Our third contribution is an empirical study demonstrating that the combined system improves the accuracy-efficiency trade-off on standard KGQA benchmarks compared to retrieval-heavy baselines and to purely symbolic pipelines. Together, these elements make multi-hop KGQA more tractable for deployments that must minimize query cost while preserving interpretable reasoning traces.\nL89: ## 2 Related Work\nL90: ### 2.1 Neural–Symbolic Integration\nL91: Neural and symbolic methods have been combined to yield systems that leverage the perceptual strength of deep networks alongside the compositionality and interpretability of symbolic rules. Early work on joint neural perception and logic reasoning established the value of end-to-end coupling between perception modules and symbolic inference engines [cite68†11 , cite69†12 , cite70†13 ].\nL92: Differentiable relaxations of logic programs enable gradient-based learning of rule weights and structure, which improves robustness on noisy or structured examples [cite71†14 , cite72†15 , cite61†4 , cite73†16 ]. More recent studies scale differentiable neuro-symbolic reasoning to large knowledge graphs and propose practical approximations that trade off efficiency and fidelity to logical constraints [cite74†17 , cite75†18 ].\nL93: Surveys and reviews summarize advances and identify central challenges such as scalability, multimodal integration, and preserving interpretability [cite76†19 , cite77†20 ]. These works provide the foundational techniques that our system adapts to the multi-hop KGQA setting.\nL94: ### 2.2 Differentiable Rule Learning and Logic Networks\nL95: Learning first-order rules through differentiable semantics has seen rapid progress. Approaches construct continuous surrogates for logical operators, enabling rules to be discovered and refined with gradient signals [cite61†4 , cite71†14 , cite73†16 ]. Architectures that produce interpretable logic networks by softening discrete components have demonstrated competitive performance while improving model transparency [cite73†16 , cite72†15 ].\nL96: Complementary lines of work study probabilistic soft logic and energy-based neuro-symbolic models that meld weighted rules with embedding-based scores [cite70†13 , cite74†17 ]. These methods inform our differentiable inductive logic layer and the techniques used to update rule confidences from supervised and human-provided signals.\nL97: ### 2.3 Human-in-the-Loop and Active Supervision\nL98: Human involvement during model training and inference can dramatically increase sample efficiency and correctness for uncertain decisions. Active learning and related human-in-the-loop paradigms provide mechanisms to query annotators selectively, with proven benefits across domains [cite78†21 , cite79†22 , cite80†23 , cite81†24 ]. The literature distinguishes active selection strategies that reduce label cost from interactive protocols that allow richer human guidance [cite78†21 , cite82†25 ].\nL99: Graph-specific active schemes, including hybrid uncertainty reduction and noisy-crowd models, are particularly relevant when queries concern graph structure or relation validity [cite65†8 , cite83†26 ]. We design our query policy and replay buffer to concentrate human effort where it yields maximal information gain, building on these established techniques.\nL100: ### 2.4 Uncertainty Quantification and Entropy Prediction\nL101: Accurate uncertainty estimates are central to effective query selection and to adaptive exploration strategies. Work on epistemic and heteroscedastic uncertainty for neural networks proposes principled estimators and Bayesian approximations that improve active-selection reliability [cite84†27 , cite85†28 , cite86†29 , cite87†30 , cite88†31 ].\nL102: For graph-structured data and few-shot KG completion, uncertainty-aware GNNs have been proposed to model distributional uncertainty and to guide sampling [cite89†32 , cite90†33 , cite91†34 ]. These techniques motivate our neural entropy predictor and the calibration methods we apply to the uncertainty module.\nL103: ### 2.5 Retrieval-Augmented Generation and RAG-DDR\nL104: Integrating retrieval components with generative models has become a practical approach to reduce model hallucinations and recover up-to-date facts [cite92†35 , cite93†36 , cite94†37 , cite95†38 ]. Recent proposals frame end-to-end optimization of retrieval-augmented systems through differentiable reward signals, aligning retrieval and generation modules for global performance gains [cite92†35 , cite96†39 , cite93†36 ].\nL105: Our adapter design and the way we condition LLMs on compact, fused path representations are informed by these retrieval and optimization strategies [cite92†35 , cite96†39 , cite94†37 ].\nL106: ### 2.6 Search, Monte Carlo Tree Search and Differentiable Exploration\nL107: Search-based methods that view multi-hop KGQA as sequential decision making have advanced with MCTS- and RL-style explorations. Training-free tree search guided by LLM evaluations yields competitive reasoning traces [cite97†40 , cite98†41 , cite99†42 ]. Differentiable relaxations for discrete selection, such as Gumbel-Softmax and straight-through estimators, enable backpropagation through exploration and selection decisions [cite100†43 , cite101†44 ].\nL108: Prior work combines structured exploration with neural guidance to improve path discovery over KGs [cite102†45 , cite103†46 , cite104†47 ]. We adopt progressive widening and a continuously relaxed rollout mechanism so that inner-loop search remains differentiable and compatible with gradient-based updates.\nL109: ### 2.7 Knowledge Graph Question Answering and Multi-hop Reasoning\nL110: Classical and modern KGQA approaches range from end-to-end embedding models to hybrid pipelines that retrieve subgraphs and apply symbolic or neural reasoning [cite105†48 , cite102†45 , cite106†49 , cite104†47 ]. The multi-hop KGQA literature emphasizes accurate subgraph retrieval, robust path scoring, and methods for mitigating spurious paths [cite107†50 , cite108†51 , cite109†52 , cite102†45 ].\nL111: Recent works explore LLMs as reasoning agents over KGs and study decomposition or conversation-based interactions to refine answers [cite110†53 , cite111†54 , cite112†55 ]. Our framework addresses the three core KGQA challenges simultaneously: targeted retrieval, symbolic plausibility scoring, and LLM-conditioned answer generation.\nL112: ### 2.8 Prompting, Adapter Strategies and Compact LLMs\nL113: Prompt-based and adapter-based conditioning of frozen LLMs provide parameter-efficient ways to incorporate structured context [cite113†56 , cite114†57 , cite115†58 , cite116†59 ]. Knowledge adapters map fused graph and symbolic signals into compact soft prompts that preserve task-relevant information while controlling token overhead. These design choices are aligned with findings that compact prompt tuning can make LLMs effectively consume external knowledge without full fine-tuning [cite114†57 , cite113†56 , cite115†58 ].\nL114: ### 2.9 Complementary Methods: Rule-Regularized Embeddings and Scalability\nL115: A number of works enhance embedding-based reasoning with logical constraints or rule regularizers to improve interpretability and generalization [cite69†12 , cite70†13 , cite75†18 ]. Scalability-oriented research studies approximations and filtering mechanisms that reduce the combinatorial cost of rule evaluation on large graphs [cite74†17 , cite75†18 , cite117†60 ]. These contributions guided our choices for selective triple filtering and for efficient batched evaluation during search.\nL116: ### 2.10 Summary of Positioning\nL117: The contributions described in this paper draw upon and synthesize multiple active research threads: differentiable logic and rule learning [cite71†14 , cite61†4 , cite73†16 , cite72†15 ], neural-symbolic fusion and soft-rule integration [cite68†11 , cite70†13 , cite74†17 ], human-in-the-loop and active supervision strategies [cite78†21 , cite65†8 , cite79†22 ], uncertainty modeling for selective querying [cite84†27 , cite89†32 , cite86†29 ], and differentiable exploration mechanisms that allow end-to-end updates through search [cite97†40 , cite100†43 , cite98†41 ].\nL118: The following sections explain how these elements are combined and extended in our NeuroSymActive framework to address multi-hop KGQA with constrained annotation budgets.\nL119: cite118†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Architectural overview of the NeuroSymActive framework for knowledge graph question answering. The framework operates via a coupled dual-loop optimization process across three main stages: Stage 1: Uncertainty-Aware Active Retrieval, which utilizes a Bayesian head to model heteroscedastic uncertainty in hop prediction and a neural entropy predictor $\\eta_{\\theta}$ to estimate information gain ($IG$).\nL120: This stage selectively invokes a Human Oracle via the $\\mathrm{QUERY\\_HUMAN}$ action when uncertainty $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathbf{s}_{t})$ exceeds the threshold $\\tau_{\\mathrm{hop}}$. Stage 2: Differentiable Neural-Symbolic Fusion, where a hybrid Knowledge Adapter merges continuous path embeddings ($\\mathbf{z}_{f}^{\\mathrm{neural}}$) with soft symbolic plausibility scores ($\\mathbf{z}_{f}^{\\mathrm{sym}}$) derived from a Differentiable Inductive Logic Layer (DILL).\nL121: Rule confidences $w_{\\rho}$ are updated via gradient signals from active supervision stored in the human replay buffer $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{human}}$. Stage 3: Differentiable MCTS with Active Exploration, which implements a relaxed Monte Carlo Tree Search. It employs Progressive Widening governed by predictive uncertainty and treats human intervention as explicit nodes with value $V_{\\mathrm{human}}(s)$.\nL122: The search is fully differentiable via Gumbel-Softmax relaxations, allowing end-to-end joint training of the policy $\\pi_{\\phi}$, value $v_{\\psi}$, and symbolic weights through a composite multi-objective loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{total}}$.\nL123: ## 3 Methodology\nL124: \nL125: This section describes the NeuroSymActive methodology for multi-hop question answering over knowledge graphs. The design preserves a retrieve–embed–reason pipeline while introducing active, differentiable retrieval, a hybrid neural-symbolic knowledge adapter, and an iterative neural-guided graph exploration mechanism that interleaves retrieval and reasoning.\nL126: ### 3.1 Problem formalization\nL127: We cast knowledge-graph question answering as a partially observable sequential decision process with an explicit neural-symbolic duality. Let $\\mathcal{G}=\\{(h,r,t)\\}$ denote a knowledge graph and let $q$ be a natural-language query. The agent maintains an internal cognitive state $\\mathbf{s}_{t}$ that decomposes into a continuous neural component $\\mathbf{s}_{t}^{\\mathrm{neural}}$ and a discrete symbolic component $\\mathbf{s}_{t}^{\\mathrm{sym}}$.\nL128: The neural component contains differentiable embeddings of currently retrieved subgraphs and related latent variables, whereas the symbolic component encodes discrete logical constraints, type relations and other symbolic checks used for pruning and consistency verification.\nL129: At each decision step $t$ the agent selects an action $a_{t}$ from a set of candidate retrieval/expansion operations $\\mathcal{A}_{t}$ augmented with an explicit human-query action $\\mathrm{QUERY\\_HUMAN}$, that is $a_{t}\\in\\mathcal{A}_{t}\\cup\\{\\mathrm{QUERY\\_HUMAN}\\}$. Selecting $\\mathrm{QUERY\\_HUMAN}$ triggers a supervised annotation with incurred cost $c_{\\mathrm{human}}>0$. An episode terminates when the agent emits a final answer $a$ or exhausts its computational or annotation budget.\nL130: The agent’s objective trades off answer correctness and human annotation cost: it seeks to maximize expected accuracy while minimizing cumulative annotation expenditure. Formally we optimize a bi-objective that can be collapsed into a single scalar objective by penalizing human queries:\nL131: \nL132:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{J}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\bigl[\\mathbb{I}[\\hat{a}=a^{\\star}]\\bigr]$  |  | (1)\nL133:  |  | $\\displaystyle-\\beta\\sum_{t}\\mathbb{I}\\bigl[a_{t}=\\mathrm{QUERY\\_HUMAN}\\bigr]\\,c_{\\mathrm{human}},$  |\nL134: where $\\hat{a}$ denotes the emitted answer, $a^{\\star}$ is the ground-truth answer, $\\mathbb{I}[\\cdot]$ is the indicator function, and $\\beta>0$ balances accuracy and annotation cost. In (cite119†1 ), the expectation is taken over the agent’s stochastic policy and any environment randomness.\nL135: \nL136: A reasoning path $p$ is represented by an ordered sequence of triples with both neural embeddings and symbolic identifiers:\nL137: \nL138:  | $$p=\\bigl((e_{0},r_{1},e_{1}),\\dots,(e_{L-1},r_{L},e_{L})\\bigr),$$  |  | (2)\nL139: where each entity $e_{i}$ and relation $r_{j}$ is associated with a continuous embedding $\\mathbf{e}_{i},\\mathbf{r}_{j}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ used by the differentiable modules and with a discrete symbolic identifier used by the symbolic checks, and $L$ denotes the dynamic path length determined by the agent’s value function or budget termination. In (cite120†2 ), the neural embedding vectors live in $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ and the symbolic identifiers index logical constraints and types.\nL140: ### 3.2 Algorithm framework\nL141: \nL142: The following algorithm summarizes the coupled inner/outer loop of NeuroSymActive in a concise form. Key estimators and relaxations are referenced where used. The overall procedure is summarized in Algorithm cite121†1 .\nL143: \nL144: Algorithm 1 NeuroSymActive\nL145: \nL146: 0:  knowledge graph $G$, query $q$, budgets, human buffer $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{human}}$\nL147: \nL148: 1:  extract anchors $B$ and compute $V_{q}=\\mathrm{PLM}(q)$\nL149: \nL150: 2:  obtain hop distribution and uncertainty via (cite122†4 )\nL151: 3:  initialize $G_{\\mathrm{cur}}\\leftarrow\\varnothing$ and search root\nL152: \nL153: 4:  while not terminated do\nL154: \nL155: 5:   if uncertainty $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathbf{s}_{t})>\\tau_{\\mathrm{human}}$ then invoke $\\mathrm{QueryHuman}$ and update rules by (cite123†16 )\nL156: \nL157: 6:   for iteration = 1 to $N_{\\mathrm{inner}}$ do\nL158: \nL159: 7:    enumerate candidate relations $\\{r\\}$ and predict $\\widehat{\\mathrm{IG}}(r)$ via (cite124†5 )\nL160: \nL161: 8:    compute utility $u(r)$ using (cite125†6 ) and obtain soft weights $\\{s_{r}\\}$ via (cite126†7 )\nL162: 9:    expand $G_{\\mathrm{cur}}$ with top/sampled $s_{r}$, compute $\\mathbf{z}_{f}^{\\mathrm{neural}}$ via (cite127†11 )\nL163: \nL164: 10:    compute symbolic activations and score via (cite128†12 )–(cite129†13 ) and fuse using (cite130†14 )\nL165: \nL166: 11:    run differentiable rollouts under tree policy $\\pi_{\\mathrm{tree}}$ in (cite131†19 ), guided by $\\mathrm{UCB}_{\\mathrm{PW}}$ in (cite132†17 ); treat human choices with value in (cite133†18 )\nL167: \nL168: 12:   end for\nL169: 13:   form $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{total}}$ per (cite134†20 ) and update $\\pi_{\\phi},v_{\\psi},$ adapter and DILL weights\nL170: \nL171: 14:  end while\nL172: \nL173: 15:  return highest-scoring answer from the LLM conditioned on the adapter prompt\nL174: #### Implementation guidance.\nL175: Use minibatched inner-loop rollouts with shared batched GNNs for $\\eta_{\\theta}$ to amortize entropy predictions. Maintain separate optimizers for DILL parameters $\\{w_{\\rho}\\}$ and neural encoder weights to allow differential learning rates. For stability, anneal the Gumbel temperature $\\tau$ in (cite126†7 ) and the search temperature $\\tau_{\\mathrm{search}}$ in (cite131†19 ), and apply gradient normalization when minimizing (cite134†20 ) so that no single objective dominates training.\nL176: ### 3.3 High-level architecture: coupled dual-loop optimization\nL177: NeuroSymActive operates through two tightly coupled loops that run on distinct time scales and exchange information continuously. The inner loop is a fast, differentiable neural-symbolic exploration driven by a neural policy $\\pi_{\\phi}$ and a value estimator $v_{\\psi}$.\nL178: At this time scale the system performs many lightweight expansions: the policy proposes candidate relation expansions, symbolic constraints encoded as a differentiable logical layer prune infeasible expansions, and discrete choices are represented with continuous relaxations (for example Gumbel-Softmax) so that gradients can flow through the exploration decisions. The inner loop thus produces a relaxed reasoning graph whose edge-selection variables are stochastic, differentiable weights.\nL179: The outer loop runs at a slower time scale and implements active human-in-the-loop supervision. An uncertainty quantifier $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathbf{s}_{t})$, which monitors entropy and saturation of information gain, decides whether to invoke $\\mathrm{QUERY\\_HUMAN}$. When the uncertainty metric exceeds a pre-specified threshold or when information gain plateaus, the system requests expert feedback for critical decisions such as hop depth, relation relevance, or path validity.\nL180: Human annotations are stored in a replay buffer $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{human}}$ and used for off-policy updates of $\\pi_{\\phi}$, $v_{\\psi}$, and the neural-symbolic adapter.\nL181: The knowledge adapter forms the bridge between modalities by fusing three signals: continuous path embeddings produced by the neural encoders, soft plausibility scores returned by the differentiable symbolic module, and uncertainty signals used to trigger outer-loop queries. The adapter projects fused representations into the frozen LLM’s token embedding space as compact soft prompts.\nL182: Gradients from the LLM’s downstream loss are routed back to the differentiable components through the adapter’s continuous interfaces while symbolic constraints remain enforced via soft scores.\nL183: ### 3.4 Stage 1: Uncertainty-aware active retrieval\nL184: \nL185: We first refine hop prediction using a probabilistic neural head that models heteroscedastic uncertainty. The semantic encoder computes\nL186: \nL187:  | $$V_{q}=\\mathrm{PLM}(q),$$  |  | (3)\nL188: \nL189: where $\\mathrm{PLM}(\\cdot)$ is a pretrained language encoder and $V_{q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ is the semantic vector for query $q$. A Bayesian head yields a distribution over hop budgets:\nL190:  | $\\displaystyle P(h_{q}\\mid V_{q})$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathrm{Softmax}\\bigl(\\mathrm{MLP}(V_{q})+\\boldsymbol{\\epsilon}\\bigr),$  |  | (4)\nL191:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\text{where }\\boldsymbol{\\epsilon}\\sim\\mathcal{N}\\bigl(\\mathbf{0},\\sigma^{2}(V_{q})\\mathbf{I}\\bigr).$  |\nL192: where $\\mathrm{MLP}(\\cdot)$ is a small feedforward network and $\\sigma^{2}(V_{q})$ is a learned heteroscedastic variance. In (cite122†4 ), the additive Gaussian noise models epistemic/aleatoric uncertainty and the softmax converts logits to a probability distribution over discrete hop counts. If $\\max_{h}P(h_{q}=h\\mid V_{q})$ falls below a threshold $\\tau_{\\mathrm{hop}}$, the system issues $\\mathrm{QueryHuman}(\\textit{hop-depth},q)$ to obtain an annotated hop count $h_{q}^{\\star}$.\nL193: Estimating the information gain for a candidate relation $r$ is computationally expensive if performed by Monte-Carlo simulation. We therefore learn a neural entropy predictor $\\eta_{\\theta}$ that maps subgraph features to predicted answer entropy and use it to approximate information gain:\nL194: \nL195:  | $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{IG}}(r)=\\eta_{\\theta}\\bigl(G_{\\mathrm{cur}},r,V_{q}\\bigr)-\\eta_{\\theta}\\bigl(G_{\\mathrm{cur}},\\varnothing,V_{q}\\bigr),$$  |  | (5)\nL196: where $\\eta_{\\theta}(\\cdot)$ is implemented as a graph neural network that ingests the current subgraph $G_{\\mathrm{cur}}$, the candidate relation $r$, and the query embedding $V_{q}$, and returns a scalar predictive entropy. In (cite124†5 ), the second term denotes predicted entropy without adding relation $r$.\nL197: \nL198: We generalize the acquisition utility to support both autonomous and human-augmented selection. The two-mode utility is\nL199:  | $$u(r)=\\left\\{\\begin{array}[]{ll}\\widehat{\\mathrm{IG}}(r)-\\lambda\\,C(r),&\\text{(auto mode)}\\\\[6.0pt]\nL200: \\widehat{\\mathrm{IG}}_{\\mathrm{human}}(r)-\\lambda\\,C(r)-c_{\\mathrm{human}},&\\text{(human)}\\end{array}\\right.$$  |  | (6)\nL201: where $C(r)$ is an estimated retrieval cost, $\\lambda>0$ balances information gain and cost, $c_{\\mathrm{human}}$ is the explicit annotation cost for invoking human feedback, and $\\widehat{\\mathrm{IG}}_{\\mathrm{human}}(\\cdot)$ leverages relevance labels from the human replay buffer $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{human}}$. In (cite125†6 ), the human-augmented mode is considered when the uncertainty quantifier or policy indicates high ambiguity at a decision point.\nL202: To allow end-to-end optimization, discrete relation selection is relaxed via a Gumbel-Softmax reparameterization. For a candidate set $\\{r\\}$ with base logits $\\log\\pi(r)$ we compute soft selection weights\nL203: \nL204:  | $\\displaystyle s_{r}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\exp\\bigl((\\log\\pi(r)+g_{r})/\\tau\\bigr)}{\\sum_{r^{\\prime}}\\exp\\bigl((\\log\\pi(r^{\\prime})+g_{r^{\\prime}})/\\tau\\bigr)},$  |  | (7)\nL205:  |  | $\\displaystyle g_{r}\\sim\\mathrm{Gumbel}(0,1),$  |\nL206: where $\\tau>0$ is a temperature parameter and $s_{r}\\in[0,1]$ approximates a one-hot selection in the low-temperature limit. In (cite126†7 ), sampling the Gumbel noise $g_{r}$ and dividing by $\\tau$ yields a differentiable approximation to categorical sampling used during inner-loop exploration and training.\nL207: ### 3.5 Stage 2: Differentiable neural-symbolic fusion\nL208: \nL209: The knowledge adapter places a differentiable inductive logic layer at the core of neural-symbolic integration so that symbolic inference participates in end-to-end gradient-based learning. The adapter receives path-level neural encodings and yields fused representations that carry both distributional semantics and logical validity.\nL210: For a sampled reasoning path $p$ we first obtain element-wise embeddings from textual and structural encoders. For the $i$-th triple in $p$ denote the relation, head entity and tail entity embeddings by\nL211: \nL212:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{e}^{r}_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathrm{Embed}(r_{i}),\\quad\\mathbf{e}^{h}_{i}=\\mathrm{Embed}(e_{i-1}),$  |  | (8)\nL213:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{e}^{t}_{i}=\\mathrm{Embed}(e_{i}),$  |\nL214: where $\\mathrm{Embed}(\\cdot)$ denotes a shared textual encoder and each vector lies in $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$. In (cite135†8 ), the symbols $\\mathbf{e}^{r}_{i}$, $\\mathbf{e}^{h}_{i}$ and $\\mathbf{e}^{t}_{i}$ denote relation, head and tail continuous embeddings respectively.\nL215: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing EVMbench | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/introducing-evmbench/)\nciteturn31956view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/introducing-evmbench/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 153\n"
